   "source": [
    "# Extended Kalman Filter"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "43297fec-3bbe-44c3-a9ce-c282598e532c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Intro\n",
    "\n",
    "This is an algorithmic tool used for estimating the unknown state of a dynamic system from a time series of noisy measurements. Consider the following scenario where you are driving a car where the GPS is starting to lose connection. You have two sources of information:\n",
    "- **Model Prediction:** You know the previous speed and direction, so you have an idea of where the card should generally be. But, wheel slip and wind creates excess noise over time.\n",
    "- **Measurement:** A sensor gives position estimates with relatively high amounts of noise.\n",
    "Note that in both scenarios, we do not get 100% reliable information. And so, the Kalman Filter acts as a priority weighting algorithm: it specifically quantifies the uncertainty between both sources, and applies a weighting factor (**Kalman Gain**) that minimizes total estimation error."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fcc012ef-1cd1-4592-8cdf-67042af7258c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Linear Model\n",
    "\n",
    "The basic pipeline follows two linear matrix equations:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{x}_k = \\mathbf{F}_k \\mathbf{x}_{k-1} + \\mathbf{B}_k \\mathbf{u}_k + \\mathbf{w}_k$$$$\\mathbf{z}_k = \\mathbf{H}_k \\mathbf{x}_k + \\mathbf{v}_k$$\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "- $\\mathbf{x_k}:$ state vector at time $k$\n",
    "- $\\mathbf{F_k}:$ state transition matrix\n",
    "- $\\mathbf{u_k}:$ control input vector\n",
    "- $\\mathbf{B_k}:$ control input matrix\n",
    "- $\\mathbf{z_k}:$ measurement vector from sensors\n",
    "- $\\mathbf{H_k}:$ measurement matrix (state space to measurement space)\n",
    "- $\\mathbf{w_k}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,\\mathbf{Q_k}):$ process noise with covariance matrix $\\mathbf{Q_k}$\n",
    "- $\\mathbf{v_k}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,\\mathbf{R_k}):$ measurement noise with covariance matrix $\\mathbf{R_k}$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5996805b-2620-4f16-b626-45f4d43f6e30",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "First we must determine estimate our time-dependent factors. We specifically must propagate state vector $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k-1\\vert{}k-1}$ and error covariance $\\mathbf{P}_{k-1\\vert{}k-1}$ forward in time:\n",
    "First, make the next predicted state estimate:\n",
    "$$\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k\\vert{}k-1} = \\mathbf{F}_k \\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k-1\\vert{}k-1} + \\mathbf{B}_k \\mathbf{u}_k$$\n",
    "And then the predicted covariance estimate:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{P}_{k\\vert{}k-1} = \\mathbf{F}_k \\mathbf{P}_{k-1\\vert{}k-1} \\mathbf{F}_k^T + \\mathbf{Q}_k$$\n",
    "Now we can correct our actual prediction using a new sensor measurement $\\mathbf{z_k}$:\n",
    "- **Measurement Error** is calculated with:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{y}_k = \\mathbf{z}_k - \\mathbf{H}_k \\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k\\vert{}k-1}$$\n",
    "- The new **Covariance Estimate** is calculated as:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{S}_k = \\mathbf{H}_k \\mathbf{P}_{k\\vert{}k-1} \\mathbf{H}_k^T + \\mathbf{R}_k$$\n",
    "- The optimal **Kalman Gain** is calculated as:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{K}_k = \\mathbf{P}_{k\\vert{}k-1} \\mathbf{H}_k^T \\mathbf{S}_k^{-1}$$\n",
    "- The updated **state estimate** is now:\n",
    "$$\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k\\vert{}k} = \\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k\\vert{}k-1} + \\mathbf{K}_k \\mathbf{y}_k$$\n",
    "- The updated **covariance estimate** is:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{P}_{k\\vert{}k} = (\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{K}_k \\mathbf{H}_k) \\mathbf{P}_{k\\vert{}k-1}$$\n",
    "\n",
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f719667c-0caa-4fdc-b237-2760baa1a17e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## The Extended Kalman Filter\n",
    "\n",
    "In the real world, systems are rarely ever linear. So, instead of using matrix multiplication, transitions and measurements are governed by non-linear functions:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{x}_k = f(\\mathbf{x}_{k-1}, \\mathbf{u}_{k-1}) + \\mathbf{w}_k$$\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{z}_k = h(\\mathbf{x}_k) + \\mathbf{v}_k$$\n",
    "But, there is something important to note here: If a Gaussian distribution passes through a non-linear function, then it's no longer Gaussian. So, we cannot apply this linear pipeline to non-linear equations, or else the filter will skew and diverge. The solution is **local linearization** through a first-order Taylor expansion. The EKF compute the first-order partial derivatives (the Jacobians of the $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{H}$ matrices):\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{F}_{k-1} = \\left. \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\mathbf{x}} \\right\\vert{}_{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k-1\\vert{}k-1}, \\mathbf{u}_{k-1}}$$$$\\mathbf{H}_k = \\left. \\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial \\mathbf{x}} \\right\\vert{}_{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k\\vert{}k-1}}$$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "eff8ad5d-7c4c-49eb-9503-b7e9cf55bbd7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## EKF Pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "The next state is predicted through a non-linear transition function, as opposed to computing it linearly with matrices:\n",
    "$$\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k\\vert{}k-1} = f(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k-1\\vert{}k-1}, \\mathbf{u}_{k-1})$$\n",
    "Our transition matrix of Jacobians is then:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{F}_{k-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}     \\frac{\\partial f_1}{\\partial x_1} & \\frac{\\partial f_1}{\\partial x_2} & \\cdots \\\\    \\frac{\\partial f_2}{\\partial x_1} & \\frac{\\partial f_2}{\\partial x_2} & \\cdots \\\\    \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots    \\end{bmatrix}_{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k-1\\vert{}k-1}}$$\n",
    "And so, propagating our error covariance is then effectively the same thing as the standard KF pipeline:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{P}_{k\\vert{}k-1} = \\mathbf{F}_{k-1} \\mathbf{P}_{k-1\\vert{}k-1} \\mathbf{F}_{k-1}^T + \\mathbf{Q}_{k-1}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Now we can move on to the actual measurement corrections:\n",
    "- The **measurement residual** is:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{y}_k = \\mathbf{z}_k - h(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k\\vert{}k-1})$$\n",
    "- The Jacobian for the **measurement matrix** is:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{H}_k = \\begin{bmatrix}     \\frac{\\partial h_1}{\\partial x_1} & \\frac{\\partial h_1}{\\partial x_2} & \\cdots \\\\    \\frac{\\partial h_2}{\\partial x_1} & \\frac{\\partial h_2}{\\partial x_2} & \\cdots \\\\    \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots    \\end{bmatrix}_{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k\\vert{}k-1}}$$\n",
    "- The **covariance estimate** is:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{S}_k = \\mathbf{H}_k \\mathbf{P}_{k\\vert{}k-1} \\mathbf{H}_k^T + \\mathbf{R}_k$$\n",
    "- The **Kalman gain** is:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{K}_k = \\mathbf{P}_{k\\vert{}k-1} \\mathbf{H}_k^T \\mathbf{S}_k^{-1}$$\n",
    "- The **state correction** is:\n",
    "$$\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k\\vert{}k} = \\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k\\vert{}k-1} + \\mathbf{K}_k \\mathbf{y}_k$$\n",
    "- The **covariance correction** is:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{P}_{k\\vert{}k} = (\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{K}_k \\mathbf{H}_k) \\mathbf{P}_{k\\vert{}k-1}$$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e4b6b420-2844-49ca-a4ca-aad2ffee9bee",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Example\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's run through an actual example to show how we calculate Heston parameters using an EKF. First, let's define the scenario. The Heston framework assumes asset shocks and additional variance shocks. So, our measurements will consist of these asset shocks, and the model/states will revolve around the variance shocks. \n",
    "\n",
    "### 0) Initial Assumptions\n",
    "\n",
    "For our base model parameters, we say:\n",
    "- Daily Time Step ($\\Delta t$): $1/252 \\approx 0.003968$\n",
    "- Mean Reversion Speed ($\\kappa$): $2.0$\n",
    "- Long-Term Variance ($\\theta$): $0.04$ ($\\sqrt{0.04} = 20\\%$ annualized vol)\n",
    "- Vol-of-Vol ($\\sigma$): $0.30$\n",
    "- Measurement Noise Variance ($R$): $\\frac{\\pi^2}{2} \\approx 4.9348$\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1) Compute Observables\n",
    "\n",
    "Our raw data is an array of closing prices. We calculate returns at a frequency of $\\Delta t = \\frac{1}{252}$$\\Delta t = \\frac{1}{252}$:\n",
    "$$r_t = \\ln\\left(\\frac{S_t}{S_{t-1}}\\right), \\quad \\text{for } t = 1, 2, \\dots, T$$\n",
    "In the Heston framework, the distribution of an asset is $r_t \\sim \\mathcal{N}\\left(\\left(\\mu - \\frac{1}{2}V_t\\right)\\Delta t, V_t \\Delta t\\right)$. Therefore, the return shock or standard deviation $\\sigma$ is $r_t = \\sqrt{V_t \\Delta t} \\cdot \\epsilon_t$ where $\\epsilon_t \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)$. In logarithmic terms, this is expressed as:\n",
    "$$\\ln(r_t^2) = \\ln(V_t \\Delta t) + \\ln(\\epsilon_t^2)$$\n",
    "Since this is just the logarithm of a standard random variable, the mean and variance are:\n",
    "$$E[\\ln(\\epsilon_t^2)] = -1.2704, \\quad \\text{Var}(\\ln(\\epsilon_t^2)) = \\frac{\\pi^2}{2} \\approx 4.9348$$\n",
    "The observable $z_t$ must be centered at zero, therefore the measurement input is:\n",
    "$$z_t = \\ln(r_t^2) + 1.2704 = \\ln(V_t \\Delta t) + \\xi_t, \\quad \\xi_t \\sim \\left(0, R = \\frac{\\pi^2}{2}\\right)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2) Prediction Phase\n",
    "\n",
    "Pass the previous state estimate through to the transition function $f(x)$:\n",
    "$$f(V_{t-1}) = V_{t-1} + \\kappa(\\theta - V_{t-1})\\Delta t$$\n",
    "Now, we take the base state transition to be the Jacobian of $f(x)$:\n",
    "$$F = \\frac{d}{dV}\\left[ V + \\kappa(\\theta - V)\\Delta t \\right] = 1 - \\kappa \\Delta t$$\n",
    "Process noise variance is then:\n",
    "$$Q_{t-1} = \\sigma^2 \\hat{V}_{t-1\\vert{}t-1} \\Delta t$$\n",
    "Note that because the standard deviation of residual asset shocks is the vol-of-vol, we can effectively use it for our covariance matrix here. And so, the predicted error covariance is:\n",
    "$$P_{1\\vert{}0} = F_0^2 P_{0\\vert{}0} + Q_0$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3) Measurement Update Phase\n",
    "\n",
    "Now we calculate our predicted measurement:\n",
    "$$h(V_t) = \\ln(V_t \\cdot \\Delta t)$$\n",
    "And then calculate the measurement difference through:\n",
    "$$y_1 = z_1 - h(\\hat{V}_{1\\vert{}0})$$\n",
    "Next we must calculate the Jacobian for the measurement:\n",
    "$$H = \\frac{d}{dV}\\left[ \\ln(V \\cdot \\Delta t) \\right] = \\frac{1}{V}$$\n",
    "And then the state uncertainty plus measurement uncertainty:\n",
    "$$S_1 = H_1^2 P_{1\\vert{}0} + \\frac{\\pi^2}{2}$$\n",
    "And then calculate the Kalman gain:\n",
    "$$K_1 = \\frac{P_{1\\vert{}0} H_1}{S_1}$$\n",
    "Our state correction is then:\n",
    "$$\\hat{V}_{1\\vert{}1} = \\hat{V}_{1\\vert{}0} + K_1 y_1$$\n",
    "And then the covariance update is:\n",
    "$$P_{1\\vert{}1} = (1 - K_1 H_1) P_{1\\vert{}0}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4) Parameter Optimization\n",
    "\n",
    "First, gather all measurement error residuals ($y_1, y_2, \\dots, y_T$) and all total uncertainties for prediction error ($S_1, S_2, \\dots, S_T$). Then, calculate the negative log-likelihood via:\n",
    "$$\\text{NLL}(\\kappa, \\theta, \\sigma) = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{t=1}^T \\left[ \\ln(2\\pi S_t) + \\frac{y_t^2}{S_t} \\right]$$\n",
    "In order to gauge how much each parameter should be shifted by, the following gradients are calculated ($\\epsilon \\approx 10^{-8}$):\n",
    "$$\\frac{\\partial \\text{NLL}}{\\partial \\kappa} \\approx \\frac{\\text{NLL}(\\kappa + \\epsilon, \\theta, \\sigma) - \\text{NLL}(\\kappa, \\theta, \\sigma)}{\\epsilon}$$$$\\frac{\\partial \\text{NLL}}{\\partial \\theta} \\approx \\frac{\\text{NLL}(\\kappa, \\theta + \\epsilon, \\sigma) - \\text{NLL}(\\kappa, \\theta, \\sigma)}{\\epsilon}$$$$\\frac{\\partial \\text{NLL}}{\\partial \\sigma} \\approx \\frac{\\text{NLL}(\\kappa, \\theta, \\sigma + \\epsilon) - \\text{NLL}(\\kappa, \\theta, \\sigma)}{\\epsilon}$$\n",
    "Forming the following gradient vector:\n",
    "$$\\nabla \\text{NLL}(\\boldsymbol{\\Theta}) = \\begin{bmatrix} \\frac{\\partial \\text{NLL}}{\\partial \\kappa} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial \\text{NLL}}{\\partial \\theta} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial \\text{NLL}}{\\partial \\sigma} \\end{bmatrix}$$\n",
    "Here, if a partial derivative is greater than zero, that increases the negative log-likelihood, decreasing overall likelihood. So, we ideally want all of our partial derivatives to be less than zero. We then use the Hessian matrix $B$ in order to gauge curvature:\n",
    "$$d = -B^{-1} \\nabla \\text{NLL}(\\boldsymbol{\\Theta})$$\n",
    "The new parameters are then updated as follows:\n",
    "$$\\begin{bmatrix} \\kappa_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\theta_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\sigma_{\\text{new}} \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} \\kappa_{\\text{old}} \\\\ \\theta_{\\text{old}} \\\\ \\sigma_{\\text{old}} \\end{bmatrix} + \\alpha \\begin{bmatrix} d_\\kappa \\\\ d_\\theta \\\\ d_\\sigma \\end{bmatrix}$$\n",
    "The reason we update by this curvature, is because it effectively moves each parameter either away from an unideal NLL or towards the ideal NLL. $\\alpha$ is determined using Wolfe's Conditions. "
   ]
  }
